ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563636 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563636 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563636 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563636 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563636 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f149eee6-2250-502c-8bd6-58d081055e65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563294 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563639 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fa331f7b-2a1a-5cc2-9b0b-49206016e7e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t+1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0e9c7e83-813e-5902-881b-22d16eab32e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563649 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+2}{t+1},\\,2{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{(2t)},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{{0}^{2}-2\\cdot 0+2}{0+1},\\,2\\cdot 1,\\,0\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the second component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)$ we used a special limit ${lim}_{u\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡u}{u}=1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{e}^{2t}-1}{t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{({e}^{2t}-1)^{′}}{(t)^{′}},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2{e}^{2t}}{1},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{0},\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0+1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨2,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 0+0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127734" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t),$ if $f(t)=⟨{e}^{t-2},\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c409cd1-fe1f-5e01-a403-dbc5d06f9573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563661 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2}⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}{e}^{t-2} & ={e}^{2-2} \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\sqrt{6-t} & =\\sqrt{6-2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{3}{t+2} & =\\frac{3}{2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)=⟨1,2,\\frac{3}{4}⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32979" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cd66931-0513-5d1c-9823-4729248ee301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563662 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{4+t}{5} & =\\frac{4+1}{5}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1} & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{(t-1)(t+1)} \\\\  & =({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{1}{t+1})\\cdot ({lim}_{t\\to 1}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{t-1}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{1+1})\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\sqrt{9t} & =\\sqrt{9(1)}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)=i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $1$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+3\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{41}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130314" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e0611fa-e2b7-5168-b829-407939c6ea09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563663 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+4g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+4{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+4[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78268" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "33768c70-a4a8-56c5-b929-58df88c6ba1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563664 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{t-1}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)^{′}}{(t-1)^{′}}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t}{t}) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t}{t})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,(\\frac{1}{t}) \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi }{1} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for the first component of ${lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)$ we used L'Hopital's Rule.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}-2t+1}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+t}{t+1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 1}\\,\\frac{{e}^{t+1}}{t+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{1}^{2}-2\\cdot 1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1}{1+1} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1+1}}{1+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+0\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563585 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32987" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37a31447-f0c5-5a53-afc7-a1edb4864b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563667 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130211" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6199f517-7214-5d4f-b510-e7e4d600a748" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563693 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69736" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{t})\\,j+{t}^{-2}\\,k \\\\ g(t) & =2\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j+{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d74654a-36dd-50ce-be7e-dc0c748c4dcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563706 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}})\\,i+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t}))\\,j-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2})\\,k, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2)\\,i-({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t})\\,j+({lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t})\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's find the limits one-by-one.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{{t}^{2}}{1-{t}^{2}}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}-1}=-1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }(1+\\frac{1}{t})=1+{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{t}=1 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{t}^{-2}={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }2=2 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{e}^{-t}=0 \\\\  & {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{t})}^{t}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) & =-1\\,i+1\\,j+0\\,k \\\\  & =-i+j, \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t) & =2\\,i+0\\,j+e\\,k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }[g(t)+2f(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to \\infty }g(t)+2{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }f(t) \\\\  & =(2\\,i+e\\,k)+2(-i+j) \\\\  & =2j+e\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69728" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86cd099b-e0a6-5dec-b63e-d3e60dda2214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563712 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1069 "q-127725" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t),$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a16b58b-bb85-5099-b69a-a964f563de29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563714 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1069 "q-32976" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a567a48-e070-587a-94df-8a2d7148a763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563716 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9ad6ae2-908f-5e67-9c92-f2b932e8cd1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563727 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}{t}^{2} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{0} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the third component is a special limit.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}1 \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{2t-1}{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\frac{2(0)-1}{{0}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)+g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1071 "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨6-{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{2}-6,\\,\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩, \\\\ g(t) & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t),\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,2t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bd1d10fc-2d48-5d8e-aa22-57b49e0e6a49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563729 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(6-{t}^{3}),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,({t}^{2}-6),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\frac{16}{{t}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6-{2}^{3},{2}^{2}-6,\\frac{16}{{2}^{3}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩ \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,(5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-t)),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,\\sqrt{6-t},\\,{lim}_{t\\to 2}\\,2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3-2),\\sqrt{6-2},2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 2}[f(t)\\times g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 2}f(t)\\times {lim}_{t\\to 2}g(t) \\\\  & =⟨-2,-2,2⟩\\times ⟨5,2,4⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 4-2\\cdot 2)\\,i-((-2)\\cdot 4-5\\cdot 2)\\,j+((-2)\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-2))\\,k \\\\  & =-12\\,i+18\\,j+6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-12,18,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130202" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)$, if $f(s)=⟨\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{s}^{3}-5⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d22af4f5-52e4-5523-b72f-519be01c5f87" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563738 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}},\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s),\\,{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5)⟩.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\frac{5}{{e}^{s-2}} & =\\frac{5}{{e}^{2-2}}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) & =3{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2s) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4-2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 2}\\,({s}^{3}-5) & ={2}^{3}-5=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 2}f(s)=⟨5,3,3⟩.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)]$ given that $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ are defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}],\\,g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-2 \\\\ \\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e00ec48a-b851-58e1-a999-f80b28436a4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563751 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the limit of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{{t}^{2}+9}{t+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{0} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡0 \\\\ \\frac{{0}^{2}+9}{0+3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t-2) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}\\frac{3t-2}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 0}{2}^{t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0-2 \\\\ \\frac{3(0)-2}{{0}^{2}-1} \\\\ {2}^{0}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use properties of limits to calculate the final result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}[f(t)\\cdot g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)\\cdot {lim}_{t\\to 0}g(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =3(-2)+0(2)+3(1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130214" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the limit $\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖,$ if $f(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+(t+2)^{2}\\,k.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2503fc5-32a5-54fb-be4e-1b9923b33c22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563752 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)i+({lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)j+({lim}_{t\\to 0}(t+2)^{2})k.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\,(t+2)^{2} & =(0+2)^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)=j+4\\,k.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖j+4\\,k‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{17}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130205" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e7a9644a-6597-5c1c-8581-2dcd08c08372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563754 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
